In the above study report we have compared various industrial and academic
 RISC-V designs.
 RISC-V ISA has been studied in order to understand the software-hardware
 interaction and hardware can be better designed so as to deal with some
 anomalies at hardware level only (like hazards, stall ) and make the compiler
 independent of the hardware, this can speed up the software development
 process.
 Then we proposed our RISCV architecture with various design blocks and
 strategies that we developed after going through various literature.
 For handling the hazards we will use data bypass/forwarding and 2-bit branch
 prediction scheme which is commonly used in lot of other RISC-V designs
 across academia.
 We will be designing this processor in the chisel Hardware descriptive
 language, and we have given some instances on how to design memory, cache
 efficiently with chisel which otherwise would have taken a lot more time
 and effort for doing the same job in Verilog HDL.
 
